<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/archaeology-reading-material-culture">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology recovers <strong>the 99% of human history that predates writing</strong> — plus
    the unrecorded everyday of historical eras. Its discipline: context over treasure, layers
    read as time, multiple physical clocks, and societies reconstructed from fragments.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="context, stratigraphy, the dating clocks, society from stuff" widget="equationStep" config={archCfg} />

  <h2>Discoveries that rewrote history</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Göbekli Tepe (1994→):</strong> monumental temples ~9600 BCE — BEFORE agriculture. Hunter-gatherers built for ritual, overturning "farm first, then temples" — the sequence of civilisation got rewritten by one site.</li>
    <li><strong>Çatalhöyük:</strong> a town without streets (roof entry) for ~9,000 people — "city" doesn't require streets, hierarchy markers are absent: every assumption about urbanism tested by excavation.</li>
    <li><strong>LIDAR found the Maya lowlands' scale (2015→):</strong> millions of structures under jungle canopy — populations revised upward dramatically; "the Maya collapse" became "the Maya transformation" as complexity re-emerged.</li>
    <li><strong>The Antikythera mechanism:</strong> a geared astronomical computer from ~100 BCE — ancient technology was not uniformly primitive; our record is a biased sample (survivorship of materials, not of capability).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Renfrew & Bahn, Archaeology: Theories, Methods and Practice; Schmidt on Göbekli Tepe; National Geographic LIDAR surveys (2018); de Grummond on Antikythera.</p>
</TopicPage>
